## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design algorithms with provable performance guarantees, handle pathological inputs gracefully, and understand trade-offs between different sorting approaches. It's particularly relevant for large-scale systems where worst-case behavior impacts reliability.

## Key Concepts
- **Introsort**: Hybrid algorithm combining quicksort's average-case efficiency with heapsort's worst-case guarantees
- **3-way partitioning**: Dutch national flag algorithm that efficiently handles duplicate keys
- **Depth monitoring**: Switches to heapsort when recursion depth exceeds 2⌊log₂(n)⌋ to maintain O(n log n) worst-case
- **Space complexity**: O(log n) stack space through tail-call optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
function introsort(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const maxDepth = Math.floor(2 * Math.log2(n || 1));

  function sort(left, right, depth) {
    if (left >= right) return;

    if (depth === 0) {
      heapSort(arr, left, right);
      return;
    }

    const [lt, gt] = threeWayPartition(arr, left, right);

    // Sort smaller partition first (tail recursion optimization)
    if (lt - left < right - gt) {
      sort(left, lt - 1, depth - 1);
      sort(gt + 1, right, depth - 1);
    } else {
      sort(gt + 1, right, depth - 1);
      sort(left, lt - 1, depth - 1);
    }
  }

  sort(0, n - 1, maxDepth);
  return arr;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses interval DP, proper subproblem decomposition, and practical optimization with prefix sums. Builds intuition for product costs over contiguous blocks.

## Key Concepts
- Interval DP on [i, j]
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums
- Bottom-up filling by increasing length; base case 0 for single element

## Code Example
```javascript
function minMergeCost(stones){
  const n = stones.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + stones[i];
  const sum = (i,j) => pref[j+1]-pref[i];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let len=2; len<=n; len++){
    for(let i=0; i+len-1<n; i++){
      const j = i+len-1;
      let best = Infinity;
      for(let k=i; k<j; k++){
        const cost = dp[i][k] + dp[k+1][j] + sum(i,j);
        if(cost < best) best = cost;
      }
      dp[i][j] = best;
    }
  }
  return n>0 ? dp[0][n-1] : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for K-ary merging (K > 2)?
- What optimizations apply if n is large and many merges share sums?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to blend topological ordering with DP to compute the longest-path in a weighted DAG and count the number of critical paths, including multiple sources and modulo arithmetic.

## Key Concepts
- Longest-path in DAG with node durations and edge setup weights
- Topological sort (Kahn) to process in dependency order
- DP for counting number of longest paths with modulo

## Code Example
```javascript
function countCriticalPaths(N, edges, dur) {
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  const incoming = Array.from({length: N}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(N).fill(0);

  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) {
    incoming[v].push([u, w]);
    indeg[v]++;
  }

  const EFT = dur.slice();
  const count = Array(N).fill(0);
  const q = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    if (indeg[i] === 0) {
      count[i] = 1;
      q.push(i);
    }
  }

  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    for (const [v, w] of incoming[u]) {
      const newEFT = EFT[u] + w + dur[v];
      if (newEFT > EFT[v]) {
        EFT[v] = newEFT;
        count[v] = count[u];
      } else if (newEFT === EFT[v]) {
        count[v] = (count[v] + count[u]) % MOD;
      }
      indeg[u]--;
      if (indeg[u] === 0) q.push(u);
    }
  }

  let maxEFT = 0, totalCritical = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    if (EFT[i] > maxEFT) {
      maxEFT = EFT[i];
      totalCritical = count[i];
    } else if (EFT[i] === maxEFT) {
      totalCritical = (totalCritical + count[i]) % MOD;
    }
  }
  return { EFT, maxEFT, criticalPaths: totalCritical };
}
```

## Complexity
- Time: O(N + M) for topological sort and DP
- Space: O(N + M) for adjacency and arrays

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests stability, memory constraints, and practical trade-offs between sorting algorithms. It asks for a concrete approach rather than a generic answer.

## Key Concepts

- Stable sorting
- Bottom-up merge sort
- Time/space complexity: O(n log n) time, O(n) extra space

## Code Example

```javascript
function stableSort(arr){
  const n = arr.length; 
  const aux = new Array(n);
  for (let width = 1; width < n; width *= 2){
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i += width * 2){
      const left = i;
      const mid = Math.min(i + width, n);
      const right = Math.min(i + width * 2, n);
      let p = left, q = mid, k = left;
      while (p < mid && q < right){
        if (arr[p].score <= arr[q].score) aux[k++] = arr[p++];
        else aux[k++] = arr[q++];
      }
      while (p < mid) aux[k++] = arr[p++];
      while (q < right) aux[k++] = arr[q++];
      for (let t = left; t < right; t++) arr[t] = aux[t];
    }
  }
  return arr;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for memory-constrained environments where n is very large?
- Compare stability guarantees and real-time performance between mergesort and quicksort in streaming data.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of in-place merging, a practical skill when space is constrained.

## Key Concepts
- In-place merging with end pointers
- Time O(m+n), space O(1)
- Edge cases: empty arrays, duplicates, negative numbers

## Code Example
```javascript
function mergeInPlace(A, m, B, n) {
  let i = m - 1, j = n - 1, k = m + n - 1;
  while (j >= 0) {
    if (i >= 0 && A[i] > B[j]) {
      A[k--] = A[i--];
    } else {
      A[k--] = B[j--];
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this with random arrays and verify sortedness?
- How would you adapt if A has no extra space and you can only rotate segments?

## Why This Is Asked

Duplicates are common in real data and inputs are often skewed. A 3-way partition quicksort directly handles the middle bucket of equal keys, avoiding repeated swaps of equal elements and preventing degeneracy.

## Key Concepts

- 3-way partitioning (Dutch national flag style) for handling duplicates
- Pivot selection strategies (median-of-three to reduce worst-case risk)
- Introsort/hybrid switching to guarantee worst-case bounds
- In-place sorting with O(log n) recursion depth

## Code Example

```javascript
function threeWayQuicksort(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  while (lo < hi) {
    let lt = lo, i = lo + 1, gt = hi;
    const pivot = a[lo];
    while (i <= gt) {
      if (a[i] < pivot) { [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
      else if (a[i] > pivot) { [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]]; gt--; }
      else { i++; }
    }
    threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, lt - 1);
    lo = gt; // tail recursion on the right side
  }
  return a;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify the pivot strategy to further reduce worst-case risk?
- How would you adapt this to a non-recursive implementation to save stack depth?
- Compare in-place 3-way quicksort with a stable sort like mergesort in terms of cache efficiency and memory usage.

## Why This Is Asked

This tests practical graph reasoning: counting shortest paths under a Dijkstra framework, handling path multiplicities without overflow, and edge-case ties.

## Key Concepts

- Dijkstra with path counting
- Tie handling on relaxation
- Complexity and edge-case handling

## Code Example

```javascript
function shortestPaths(graph, s, t) {
  const N = graph.length;
  const dist = Array(N).fill(Infinity);
  const ways = Array(N).fill(0);
  dist[s] = 0;
  ways[s] = 1;
  const pq = new MinHeap((a,b)=>a.d-b.d);
  pq.push({node:s, d:0});
  while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
     const {node:u, d} = pq.pop();
     if (d !== dist[u]) continue;
     for (const [v, w] of graph[u]) {
        const nd = dist[u] + w;
        if (nd < dist[v]) { dist[v] = nd; ways[v] = ways[u]; pq.push({node:v, d:nd}); }
        else if (nd === dist[v]) { ways[v] += ways[u]; }
     }
  }
  return { dist: dist[t], count: ways[t] };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you apply modulo to prevent overflow for large counts?
- How would you adapt if edges had zero weight or negative cycles are possible?

